U='https://www.westlakepipe.com/sites/default/files/2025-10/mu-ig-013-us-en-1025.1_c900-pvc-c909-pvco.pdf'
S='Westlake Pipe & Fittings, Installation Guide for C900 PVC and C909 PVCO pressure pipe, MU-IG-013-US-EN-1025.1, 2025; and Municipal Installation Guide MU-IG-017, 2025 (our summary; read 2026-11-20). Both follow AWWA C605.'
add('mfr-westlake-c900-1','US','C900 PVC water pipe installation (AWWA C605): trench, cover, joints, bending, testing rules',
"C900 PVC pressure pipe installation, summarized from Westlake Pipe's 2025 installation guides, which follow AWWA C605 (the PVC pressure pipe installation standard) and AWWA manual M23. Trench: clear width at the pipe springline at least 18 inches or pipe OD plus 1 foot, whichever is greater; no more than pipe diameter plus 2 feet at the crown; 4 inches of soil cushion where there is rock. Embedment: work material under the haunches, particle size 3/4 inch or smaller, at least 1 foot of initial backfill over the crown, compacted to what the engineer specifies. Cover: 24 inches under heavy traffic (ASTM D2774). Flotation: about 1.5 pipe diameters of cover. Joints: clean, check the gasket, lubricate the spigot bevel and the gasket with potable-safe lubricant, keep the lengths straight and push to the insertion line without over-inserting. Joint deflection for this pipe is only 1 degree (about 4-3/16 inches of offset on a 20-foot length). Bending the barrel instead is allowed by hand in 12 inch and smaller: minimum radius about 100 feet for 4 inch, 144 feet for 6 inch, 189 feet for 8 inch, 231 feet for 10 inch, 275 feet for 12 inch; never bend and deflect the same length. PVC grows or shrinks about 0.36 inch per 100 feet for each 10 F. Field cuts: square, bevel, re-mark the insertion line. No direct taps on C909 PVCO pipe. Testing: water only, never air; fill at 1 foot per second or less and vent high points; never test above the pipe's pressure rating; disinfect per AWWA C651. Up to two years of outdoor sun exposure is acceptable. Other makers' limits differ, so use the guide for the pipe on the truck.",S,U,'2025')

U='https://images.thdstatic.com/catalog/pdfImages/55/5515bbf2-b7ff-4f77-b3f6-c061e3e6beec.pdf'
S='JM Eagle (J-M Manufacturing), Solvent Weld and Electrical Conduit Installation Guide, JME-07B, revised March 2011 (our summary; read 2026-11-20)'
add('mfr-jmeagle-solvent-1','US','JM Eagle solvent-weld PVC pipe and conduit: trench, cover, cement set times (JME-07B)',
"Solvent-weld PVC pressure pipe and PVC electrical conduit, summarized from JM Eagle's installation guide JME-07B (revised March 2011). Bedding 4 to 6 inches compacted, with bell holes; on rock, a cushion of at least 4 inches of sand or select backfill (6 inches in rock cuts). Haunching and initial backfill by hand, no rocks over 1.5 inches, tamped in layers of 6 inches or less, to at least 1 foot above the pipe; 90 percent density under pavement. Trench width: pipe OD plus 1 foot minimum at the springline, pipe OD plus 2 feet maximum at the top of the pipe. Cover: 12 inches where frost is not a concern, otherwise 6 inches below the deepest recorded frost. No rolling equipment until 18 inches of backfill is over the pipe; hydro-hammers need at least 3 feet. Solvent joints: dry fit (pipe should go 1/3 to 3/4 of the way into the socket), prime, cement both parts, push home with a quarter turn, look for a full bead. Minimum set time before handling: 30 minutes at 60 to 100 F, 1 hour at 40 to 60 F, 2 hours at 20 to 40 F, 4 hours at 0 to 20 F. No deflection at solvent joints: make curves by bending the pipe; snake small pipe in the trench. Pressure test is generally 25 percent above normal operating pressure and never above the pipe's rating. Never use compressed air or gas in PVC pressure pipe. JM Eagle's gravity sewer guide is JME-05B.",S,U,'2011')

U='https://conteches.com/media/uyslalti/cmp-installation-guide.pdf'
S='Contech Engineered Solutions, CMP Installation Guide, June 2023 (our summary; read 2026-11-20)'
add('mfr-contech-cmp-1','US','Corrugated metal pipe (CMP) installation: bedding, 8-inch lifts, 90 percent, balanced backfill, cover (Contech)',
"Corrugated metal pipe installation, summarized from Contech's CMP Installation Guide (June 2023). Bedding: 4 to 6 inches of well-graded granular material on a firm foundation; dig out soft spots and replace with granular fill. Backfill: angular, well-graded granular soil (AASHTO A-1, A-2 or A-3), nothing over 3 inches, placed in loose lifts of about 8 inches and compacted to 90 percent of AASHTO T99 (95 percent in the haunches of pipe-arch). Work material into the haunches with a shovel or rod. Keep the two sides balanced: never let one side get more than 24 inches higher than the other, including between barrels. Use flowable fill (CLSM) in thin lifts where barrels are too close to compact, letting each lift set so the pipe does not float. Minimum cover for highway loads is span divided by 8 (not less than 12 inches in common practice), in place and compacted before traffic. Light tracked equipment such as a small dozer needs at least 12 inches of compacted cover; heavy construction equipment needs more per the guide's tables. Lay from the downstream end and build the outlet first so rain can drain. Unload with forks or non-metallic slings; never dump pipe off the truck or lift bundles by the strapping.",S,U,'2023')

U='https://www.ncpi.org/assets/NCPI-Bedding-Tools-Sheet-2021_090721.pdf'
S='National Clay Pipe Institute bedding sheet (2021), load factors from ASTM C12, Standard Practice for Installing Vitrified Clay Pipe Lines (our summary; read 2026-11-20)'
add('std-c12-clay-1','US','Clay pipe (VCP) bedding classes and load factors, ASTM C12',
"Vitrified clay pipe is installed under ASTM C12. Clay is rigid pipe, so the bedding class sets how much load the pipe can carry: the pipe's bearing strength is multiplied by the bedding load factor. Load factors published by the National Clay Pipe Institute from ASTM C12: Class D (flat trench bottom, least support) 1.1; Class C 1.5; Class B 1.9; crushed stone encasement 2.2; controlled low-strength material (CLSM, flowable fill) 2.8; concrete cradle 3.4. The plans call out the class; a crew that builds Class C where Class B was designed has cut the pipe's safe load by about a fifth. Shape bell holes so the barrel, not the bell, carries the load, and haunch the pipe by shovel slicing. Ten States Standards (33.83) point to these same ASTM C12 classes for rigid sewer pipe.",S,U,'2021','medium')
